const ERA_INTRO = {
 ancient:"Art begins as a job: keeping gods fed, kings remembered and the dead supplied. Watch the human figure go from rigid symbol in Mesopotamia and Egypt to breathing, moving body in Greece and Rome.",
 asia:"Two great traditions where writing, faith and craft are inseparable. In China and Japan the brush and the scroll rank above sculpture; in the Islamic world, calligraphy and pattern carry the sacred, and whole rooms become works of art.",
 aoa:"Objects made to act in the world: to honor ancestors, seal oaths, display royal power or mark a death. Many were made to be used, worn or carried rather than looked at in a gallery, so try to imagine them in motion.",
 medren:"From gold and silver for emperors to the rediscovery of perspective and oil paint. By 1600 painting has learned to make a flat panel look like a window, and artists are becoming famous names.",
 baroque:"Drama, darkness and light. Painters make you a participant in the scene, the Dutch turn ordinary rooms into masterpieces, and by the 1780s Neoclassical painting is preaching virtue on the eve of revolution.",
 c19:"Painting breaks free of the Academy. Realists paint what they see, Impressionists paint light and modern life, and Post-Impressionists bend color and form toward the 20th century.",
 us:"A young country looking for its own subjects: vast landscapes, national myths, portraits that hold their own in Paris, and by 1900 a new American design.",
 modern:"Artists stop describing the world and start reinventing how pictures work. Most of these hang in the modern wing, which is closed until 2030, so they turn up in temporary shows."
};
